{"author":{"id":"249c5793827ca0939286dfdbd6c35d9aabfb778e27ec8eab6c06481033d4a9ab"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790990903,"nonce":"Q4o9d8SyXVc4rEB3CvROdoHphdM=","title":"a host that links the mobile archive cannot hand the backend its event loop","message":"The iOS and Android releases ship libhlwindow.a for an app that links HashLink and its libraries statically. xwindow's host hook, re-exported as hlwindow::attach, is a Rust function taking a winit EventLoop, so only a Rust host that depends on hlwindow as a crate can call it. A host that links the archive from C has no entry point for it:\n\n- iOS: winit cannot pump, so nothing opens a window until a host runs the loop with attach(.., Drive::Turns(..)). A C entry such as `hlwindow_run(int (*turn)(void *), void *data)`, which builds the loop and runs it, giving the program its turns, would cover it.\n- Android: the archive carries android-activity's NativeActivity glue, which exports ANativeActivity_onCreate and calls a Rust `android_main(AndroidApp)` that the archive does not define, so a C host's app does not link. hlwindow could define android_main itself, attach the loop built with the AndroidApp, and call a C function the host provides as the program's main.\n\nEither shape is an adapter contract shared with Caribou's cb_window (caribou 1aec1814c7f4f9cb71e9aefc89bc6e817e1356ee53129f80d63093bae8f76e6e) and depends on how Ash and HashLink start a program on a phone.","files":null}]}